{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Designing High-Concurrency, High-Availability Cloud Services (Go Edition)","content":"# Designing High-Concurrency, High-Availability Cloud Services (Go Edition)\n\nUsing a Go cloud service as the example, this post systematically covers how to design, implement, and test a production-grade backend that handles high concurrency with high availability. Beyond architectural ideas, it also gives practical engineering advice around common middleware (Kafka / Redis / Postgres / etcd / Kubernetes, etc.).\n\n## 1. What is high concurrency[ #](#1-what-is-high-concurrency)\n\n**High concurrency** is a system's ability to handle a large number of requests at the same time within a unit of time.\n\nCommon metrics:\n\n* **QPS / TPS**: requests / transactions per second\n* **Concurrent connections**: requests in flight at the same time\n* **Latency**: P95 / P99\n* **Throughput**\n\nThe essence of high concurrency is not \"being fast\" but:\n\n> **How to schedule CPU, memory, I/O, and network efficiently with limited resources.**\n\n### Go is a natural fit for high concurrency[ #](#go-is-a-natural-fit-for-high-concurrency)\n\n* goroutines: an extremely low-cost concurrency model\n* channels: safe concurrent communication\n* the scheduler (GMP model): avoids thread explosion\n* rich networking libraries (net/http, grpc)\n\n## 2. What is high availability[ #](#2-what-is-high-availability)\n\n**High availability** is about:\n\n> **Whether the system can keep serving when failures occur.**\n\nCommon metrics:\n\n* **Availability SLA**: 99.9% / 99.99%\n* **MTBF / MTTR**: mean time between failures / mean time to recovery\n\nHigh availability focuses on:\n\n* Fault isolation\n* Automatic recovery\n* Degradation and fallbacks\n\nIn one sentence:\n\n> **High concurrency is about \"how busy it is\"; high availability is about \"whether it goes down\".**\n\n## 3. Achieving high concurrency[ #](#3-achieving-high-concurrency)\n\n### 1. Asynchronous design (the core idea)[ #](#1-asynchronous-design-the-core-idea)\n\nSynchronous calls:\n\n![Synchronous call](/post/highconcuravabackendingo/2099ac4a68ef.png)\n\nAsynchronous:\n\n![Asynchronous call](/post/highconcuravabackendingo/0c96b15de337.png)\n\nGood candidates for async:\n\n* Sending notifications after an order is placed\n* Writing logs / behavioral tracking events\n* Financial reconciliation\n* Data synchronization\n\n### 2. Message queues: Kafka / RocketMQ[ #](#2-message-queues-kafka--rocketmq)\n\n**Purpose**:\n\n* Smoothing traffic peaks\n* Decoupling services\n* Asynchronous processing\n\nComparison:\n\n| Aspect       | Kafka                 | RocketMQ            |\n| ------------ | --------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Throughput   | Extremely high        | High                |\n| Ordering     | Ordered per partition | Strict ordering     |\n| Transactions | Weak                  | Strong              |\n| Operations   | Fairly complex        | Relatively friendly |\n\nCommon Go clients:\n\n* sarama (Kafka)\n* rocketmq-client-go\n\n### 3. Redis: the core of high-concurrency caching[ #](#3-redis-the-core-of-high-concurrency-caching)\n\n#### 3.1 Expiration strategies[ #](#31-expiration-strategies)\n\n* Periodic deletion\n* Lazy deletion\n\n#### 3.2 Eviction policies[ #](#32-eviction-policies)\n\n* volatile-lru\n* allkeys-lru (recommended)\n* volatile-ttl\n\n#### 3.3 Sentinel vs Cluster[ #](#33-sentinel-vs-cluster)\n\n| Mode     | Use case                      | Characteristics                    |\n| -------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| Sentinel | High availability             | Automatic primary/replica failover |\n| Cluster  | High concurrency + large data | Sharding, strong scalability       |\n\n> **For read-heavy, high-concurrency workloads, Redis Cluster is the standard choice.**\n\n### 4. Database: Postgres design for high concurrency[ #](#4-database-postgres-design-for-high-concurrency)\n\n#### 4.1 Index design[ #](#41-index-design)\n\n* B-Tree (default)\n* Covering indexes\n* Avoid implicit type conversions\n\n#### 4.2 Sharding / partitioning[ #](#42-sharding--partitioning)\n\n* Partition by time (logs, orders)\n* Split tables by business dimension\n\nPostgres natively supports **partitioned tables**, which beat manual table splitting.\n\n#### 4.3 GORM usage tips[ #](#43-gorm-usage-tips)\n\n* Forbid full table scans\n* Select columns explicitly\n* Use prepared statements\n* Control the connection pool\n\n### 5. Monolith vs microservices (from a concurrency perspective)[ #](#5-monolith-vs-microservices-from-a-concurrency-perspective)\n\n| Architecture  | Pros                     | Cons                         |\n| ------------- | ------------------------ | ---------------------------- |\n| Monolith      | Simple, good performance | Hard to scale                |\n| Microservices | Easy to scale, isolation | Network overhead, complexity |\n\nA high-concurrency system **doesn't necessarily need microservices**, but it must have:\n\n* Decoupled modules\n* Clear interfaces\n\n## 4. Achieving high availability[ #](#4-achieving-high-availability)\n\n### 1. Multiple replicas and load balancing[ #](#1-multiple-replicas-and-load-balancing)\n\n#### Load balancing options[ #](#load-balancing-options)\n\n| Option         | Notes             |\n| -------------- | ----------------- |\n| NGINX          | Mature, flexible  |\n| AWS ALB/NLB    | Managed, stable   |\n| K8s Service    | Native, automatic |\n| Docker Replica | Basic scaling     |\n\n### 2. etcd: configuration and service discovery[ #](#2-etcd-configuration-and-service-discovery)\n\n* Strong consistency (Raft)\n* Configuration center\n* Service registration and discovery\n\nCommon uses:\n\n* Dynamic configuration\n* Distributed locks\n* Leader election\n\n### 3. Microservice design (DDD + gRPC)[ #](#3-microservice-design-ddd--grpc)\n\n#### Core ideas of DDD[ #](#core-ideas-of-ddd)\n\n* Bounded Context\n* Aggregate root\n* Domain events\n\n#### Advantages of gRPC + go-micro[ #](#advantages-of-grpc--go-micro)\n\n* High performance\n* Built-in service discovery\n* A mature middleware ecosystem\n\n## 5. Observability: an opaque system is an unavailable system[ #](#5-observability-an-opaque-system-is-an-unavailable-system)\n\n### 1. Log collection[ #](#1-log-collection)\n\n* Grafana Loki (lightweight)\n* Graylog (full-text search)\n\n### 2. Tracing[ #](#2-tracing)\n\n* Jaeger\n* OpenTelemetry\n\n### 3. Metrics monitoring[ #](#3-metrics-monitoring)\n\n* Prometheus\n* Grafana Dashboard\n\nKey metrics:\n\n* QPS\n* Error Rate\n* P99 Latency\n* CPU / Memory\n\n### 4. Alerting and health checks[ #](#4-alerting-and-health-checks)\n\n* Grafana AlertManager\n* UptimeRobot\n* Kubernetes Liveness / Readiness Probe\n\n## 6. Testing high concurrency[ #](#6-testing-high-concurrency)\n\n### 1. Load testing tools[ #](#1-load-testing-tools)\n\n* wrk\n* hey\n* k6\n\n### 2. Load testing principles[ #](#2-load-testing-principles)\n\n* Ramp up load gradually\n* Find the bottleneck\n* Verify the cache hit rate\n\n### 3. Common bottlenecks[ #](#3-common-bottlenecks)\n\n* Database connection pool\n* Lock contention\n* GC\n* Network I/O\n\n## 7. Testing high availability[ #](#7-testing-high-availability)\n\n### Failure drills (Chaos Engineering)[ #](#failure-drills-chaos-engineering)\n\n* Kill Pods\n* Inject network latency\n* Take Redis / the DB down\n\nTools:\n\n* Chaos Mesh\n* Manual drills\n\n## 8. Fallbacks: what if the service goes down?[ #](#8-fallbacks-what-if-the-service-goes-down)\n\n### 1. Degradation[ #](#1-degradation)\n\n* Turn off non-core features\n* Return fallback data\n\n### 2. Rate limiting[ #](#2-rate-limiting)\n\n* Token Bucket\n* Leaky Bucket\n* Redis / Envoy / NGINX\n\n### 3. Circuit breaking[ #](#3-circuit-breaking)\n\n* Triggered by error rate\n* Automatic recovery\n\n### 4. Disaster recovery[ #](#4-disaster-recovery)\n\n* Multi-AZ deployment\n* Data backups\n* Hot/cold failover\n\n## 9. Summary[ #](#9-summary)\n\nA truly **high-concurrency, high-availability Go cloud service** isn't built by piling up any single piece of middleware; it is:\n\n> The combined result of **architecture design + engineering standards + automated operations + failure awareness**.\n\nIf you remember only one thing:\n\n> **First make sure it doesn't go down, then make it faster.**\n","locale":"en","slug":"highconcuravabackendingo"}}